<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lehnin Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1180</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lehnin Abbey is a former Cistercian monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 1180 by Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a>. One of the oldest monasteries in the Mark, it was a burial place of the Ascanian margraves and a centre of German settlement and land clearance. It was dissolved after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1542, and its brick church survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lehnin-abbey.jpg" alt="Lehnin Abbey" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Savin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PM08-13_img03_Kloster_Lehnin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
